Express Architecture

The key idea:
    Request
    ↓
    Route
    ↓
    Middleware
    ↓
    Controller
    ↓
    Service
    ↓
    Repository / Model
    ↓
    Database
    ↓
    Response


1. Why not put everything in the route?
    Bad:
        app.post("/users", async (req, res) => {
            const { name, email } = req.body;

            if (!name || !email) {
                return res.status(400).json({
                    message: "Invalid data"
                });
            }

            const existingUser = await User.findOne({ email });

            if (existingUser) {
                return res.status(409).json({
                    message: "Email already exists"
                });
            }

            const user = await User.create({
                name,
                email
            });

            res.status(201).json(user);
        });

    It works, but as the application grows, routes become huge.
    You end up with:
        Routes
        ├── validation
        ├── authentication
        ├── business logic
        ├── database queries
        ├── error handling
        └── response formatting

    Hard to maintain and test.


2. Better architecture
Separate responsibilities:
    routes/
    controllers/
    services/
    repositories/
    models/
    middlewares/
    utils/

Example:
    src/
    ├── routes/
    │   └── user.routes.js
    ├── controllers/
    │   └── user.controller.js
    ├── services/
    │   └── user.service.js
    ├── repositories/
    │   └── user.repository.js
    ├── models/
    │   └── user.model.js
    ├── middlewares/
    │   ├── auth.js
    │   └── error.js
    ├── utils/
    │   └── response.js
    └── app.js


3. Routes
    Routes define:
        Which endpoint maps to which handler?
            router.post(
                "/users",
                validateUser,
                userController.createUser
            );

    The route should generally remain thin.
    It shouldn't contain your business logic.


4. Controller
    The controller handles the HTTP layer.
    It deals with:
        req
        res
        HTTP status codes
        request parameters
        calling service

    Example:
        const createUser = async (req, res, next) => {
            try {
                const user = await userService.createUser(req.body);

                res.status(201).json({
                    data: user
                });
            } catch (error) {
                next(error);
            }
        };

    The controller says:
        "I received an HTTP request. I'll pass the relevant data to the business layer and return an HTTP response."


5. Service
    The service contains business logic.

    Example:
        const createUser = async (data) => {
            const existingUser =
                await userRepository.findByEmail(data.email);

            if (existingUser) {
                throw new Error("Email already exists");
            }

            return userRepository.create(data);
        };

    The service shouldn't care whether the request came from:
        REST API
        GraphQL
        CLI
        background job

    Its job is business logic.


6. Repository
    Repository handles data access.

    Example:
        const findByEmail = (email) => {
            return User.findOne({ email });
        };

        const create = (data) => {
            return User.create(data);
        };

    The repository abstracts database operations.
    Conceptually:
        Service
        ↓
        Repository
        ↓
        Database


7. Model
    The model represents the database structure/ORM/ODM mapping.

    For Mongoose:
        const userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
            name: String,
            email: {
                type: String,
                unique: true
            }
        });

        const User = mongoose.model("User", userSchema);


8. Complete flow
    Suppose:
        POST /api/users

    Request:
        {
            "name": "John",
            "email": "john@example.com"
        }

    Flow:
        POST /api/users
                ↓
            Router
                ↓
        Middleware
                ↓
            Controller
                ↓
            Service
                ↓
        Repository
                ↓
            Model
                ↓
            Database

    Response travels back:
        Database
        ↓
        Repository
        ↓
        Service
        ↓
        Controller
        ↓
        HTTP Response


9. Why Service layer?
    Suppose you have:
        createUser()
    and the same business operation is needed by:
        REST API
        Admin API
        Background job
        CLI

    If business logic lives inside the controller, you'll duplicate it.
    Instead:
              User Service
             /     |      \
            /      |       \
        REST     Admin     Job

    Now business logic is reusable.

    Interview answer
        "I keep business logic in the service layer so it isn't tightly coupled to Express or the HTTP layer. This improves separation of concerns, reusability, and testability."


10. Controller vs Service
    Controller:
        Concerned with:
            HTTP
            req
            res
            status codes
            headers

    Service:
        Concerned with:
            Business rules
            Business operations
            Application logic

    Example:
    Controller
        const createOrder = async (req, res, next) => {
            try {
                const order = await orderService.createOrder(
                    req.user.id,
                    req.body
                );

                res.status(201).json(order);
            } catch (err) {
                next(err);
            }
        };

    Service:
        const createOrder = async (userId, data) => {
            // Check stock
            // Calculate total
            // Apply business rules
            // Create order
        };


11. Should the service use req and res?
    Generally no.

    Avoid:
        const createUser = async (req, res) => {
            // business logic
        };

    Instead:
        const createUser = async (userData) => {
            // business logic
        };

    This keeps the service independent of Express.


12. Middleware placement
    A typical request pipeline:
        Request
        ↓
        Logger
        ↓
        CORS
        ↓
        Body Parser
        ↓
        Authentication
        ↓
        Authorization
        ↓
        Validation
        ↓
        Controller
        ↓
        Service
        ↓
        Database

    Not every application needs every middleware, and exact ordering depends on the middleware's purpose.


13. Error handling
    Don't repeat this everywhere:
        try {
        ...
        } catch (err) {
        res.status(500).json(...);
        }

    You can centralize error handling.

    Controller:
        const createUser = async (req, res, next) => {
            try {
                const user = await userService.createUser(req.body);

                res.status(201).json(user);
            } catch (error) {
                next(error);
            }
        };

    Error middleware:
        app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
            console.error(err);

            res.status(500).json({
                message: "Internal Server Error"
            });
        });

    Remember:
        Error middleware must have four parameters.
        (err, req, res, next)

    And it should generally be registered after your routes.


14. Don't over-engineer
    You don't always need:
        route
        controller
        service
        repository
        factory
        adapter
        facade
        strategy
        ...

    for a tiny application.

    Architecture should match complexity.

    For a small project:
        routes → controller → database

    may be enough.

    For a larger system:
        routes
        ↓
        controllers
        ↓
        services
        ↓
        repositories
        ↓
        database

    can provide useful separation.

    Interview answer
        "I prefer separation of concerns, but I don't introduce abstractions purely for the sake of architecture. The structure should reflect the application's complexity and testing requirements."


