16. app.js vs server.js
    A good practice is separating Express configuration from server startup.
    app.js:
        const express = require("express");

        const app = express();

        app.use(express.json());

        app.use("/api/users", userRoutes);

        module.exports = app;

    server.js:
        const app = require("./app");

        const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

        app.listen(PORT, () => {
            console.log(`Server running on ${PORT}`);
        });

    Why?
        Because testing becomes easier:
        app.js
        ↓
        Express application

        server.js
        ↓
        Starts actual HTTP server

    You can import app into tests without automatically opening a network port.


17. Common interview scenario
    Interviewer:
        "Your controller has 300 lines of business logic. How would you improve it?"

    Good answer:
        "I'd keep the controller focused on HTTP concerns and move business logic into services. Database access can be isolated behind repositories or data-access modules. I'd also centralize error handling and validation where appropriate."


18. Another scenario
    "Where should database queries live?"
        There isn't one universal rule, but in a layered architecture:
            Controller
                ↓
            Service
                ↓
            Repository / Data Access
                ↓
            Database

    The repository/data-access layer owns persistence operations, while the service owns business rules.


19. Interview questions
    Q1. Controller vs Service?
        Controller = HTTP layer.
        Service = business logic.

    Q2. Why separate routes and controllers?
        To keep routing configuration separate from request-handling logic.

    Q3. Why use a repository?
        To isolate data-access logic from business logic and make persistence concerns easier to test/change.

    Q4. Should service receive req/res?
        Generally no. Pass domain/application data instead.

    Q5. Why separate app.js and server.js?
        Better separation and easier testing.

    Q6. Where should error handling happen?
        Use centralized error middleware for consistent handling, while services/controllers can throw or forward meaningful errors.

    Q7. Is repository pattern mandatory?
        No. It's an architectural choice. For simple applications it may add unnecessary abstraction.



Interview-ready architecture answer
    If an interviewer asks:
        "Explain the architecture of your Node.js application."

    You can say:
        "I typically follow a layered architecture. Routes define endpoints and middleware handles cross-cutting concerns such as authentication and validation. Controllers handle HTTP-specific concerns and delegate business logic to services. Services contain business rules and call the repository or data-access layer for database operations. Errors are handled centrally through error middleware. This keeps responsibilities separated and makes the application easier to test and maintain."


Remember
            ROUTE
            "What endpoint?"

                ↓

            MIDDLEWARE
            "Should this request proceed?"

                ↓

            CONTROLLER
            "How do I handle HTTP?"

                ↓

            SERVICE
            "What is the business rule?"

                ↓

            REPOSITORY
            "How do I access data?"

                ↓

            DATABASE
            "Where is the data?"